<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kedarnath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kedarnath is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage town in Rudraprayag district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the source of the Mandakini river at about 3,580 metres. Its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> is one of the twelve jyotirlingas and one of the Char Dham sites. Floods in 2013 devastated the town, though the stone temple survived.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kedarnath.jpg" alt="Kedarnath" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samadolfo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kedarnath_View1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kedarnath lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> about 3,583 metres above sea level, on a barren stretch of land beside the Mandakini River, near the Chorabari Glacier. It is 223 kilometres from Rishikesh and about 86.5 kilometres from Rudraprayag, and the nearest roadhead is at Gaurikund, about 16 kilometres away. Behind the town and its temple stand Kedarnath peak at 6,940 metres and the Kedar Dome at 6,831 metres. Heavy snow closes the temple in winter. From November to April the palanquin carrying the idol of Kedarnath is taken to the Omkareshwar Temple in Ukhimath, and priests and summer residents move to nearby villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name means Lord of the Field, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> kedara, field, and natha, lord. The Kashi Kedara Mahatmya says that the crop of liberation grows here. The construction of the temple is credited to the Pandava brothers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, though that epic names no place called Kedarnath. One of the earliest references is in the Skanda Purana, from about the 7th or 8th century, which names Kedara as the place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> released the waters of the Ganga from his matted hair, so forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>.</p><p>The temple is said to have been rebuilt by Adi Shankaracharya. Hagiographies based on Madhava's Sankshepa-Shankara-Vijaya say the 8th-century philosopher died near the Kedarnath mountains, while others, based on Anandagiri's Prachina-Shankara-Vijaya, say he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. The ruins of a monument to his purported resting place stand at Kedarnath. By the 12th century the site was a prominent pilgrimage centre, mentioned in the Kritya-kalpataru of the Gahadavala minister Bhatta Lakshmidhara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kedarnath is a town in Rudraprayag district and the most remote of the four Chota Char Dham pilgrimage sites. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded a population of 830, though the numbers of pilgrims between May and October run into thousands a day. Nearby are the Bhairavnath Temple, whose deity is believed to protect the town in winter, the glacier-lake Chorabari Tal, the Kedarnath Wildlife Sanctuary and the Adi Shankaracharya Samadhi. On 16 and 17 June 2013 flash floods destroyed much of the town and killed thousands of people, but the temple itself survived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kedarnath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom controlled the routes to Badrinath and <b>Kedarnath</b> and received pilgrims from across India.</blockquote><a href="/state/garhwal-kingdom">Garhwal Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
